+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The server delivers committed revisions through self-hosted WebSocket connections, avoiding dependence on a hosted realtime provider while supporting low-latency updates.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery form one synchronization path. The change store retains replayable event revisions and publishes committed revisions reliably. Delivery manages socket capacity, sessions, and publication. Browsers reconnect after a connection failure and catch up from their last applied revision before treating the board as current.
 
